# ⚠ CORRECTION — READ THIS BEFORE USING THE REPORT BELOW

**Added by lead-20 at 2026-10-07T10:15Z, after the report was committed (`a2fd18e329`).**

## ⚠⚠ SECOND, INDEPENDENT REASON THIS READING IS NOT VALID FOR THE REPO

**The measured object was NOT the repository's object — even at the time it was measured.**

```
The worker's perf-site used a SENTINEL-MODIFIED copy of templates/macros/page-navigation.html
  · required to make the build succeed at all (the withdrawn variant crashed on missing keys)
  · the same sentinel fix was applied to BOTH A and B, so the A/B *difference* is still one-line
    (18.8 MB vs empty JSON) — the delta is meaningful
  · but the *absolute* times are of a template that is NOT byte-identical to any repo version
⇒ Therefore: the delta (65.4 ms/page) describes the load cost of that variant;
  the absolute times (162.3 s / 71.5 s) describe a template that exists nowhere in the repo.
```
**Why this is placed at the TOP and not in a trailing "unverified" list:** a reader who sees the report
without this would reasonably assume the measured template is the one in the repo. It is not, and that
difference is a **precondition of the measurement's validity**, not a footnote.

**Rule (same family as the rest of this file):** before measuring, confirm the object being measured is
*currently on disk* AND *the one you claim it is*.

---

## The report measured an implementation that was NEVER COMMITTED

```
templates/macros/page-navigation.html  — every committed version references ONLY
                                          data/navigation.json (152 KB)
  cc39aaf811  10-01 21:34:25  ⇒ navigation.json (152 KB)
  cb7b700718  10-01 15:19:41  ⇒ navigation.json (152 KB)
  b3ed3b1d87  08-03 18:48:15  ⇒ navigation.json (152 KB)
  HEAD (now)                  ⇒ navigation.json (152 KB)
templates/ working tree = clean (git status --porcelain -- templates/ = empty)
```
**⇒ The variant this report measured — `load_data(path="data/page-navigation.json")` (18.8 MB) as the
macro's first statement — existed only as an UNCOMMITTED WORKING-TREE STATE (the withdrawn "R2"
implementation). It is not in any commit.**

## What therefore does NOT hold for the current implementation

| claim in this report | status |
|---|---|
| macro line 10 loads 18.8 MB `page-navigation.json` unconditionally | **INVALID for HEAD** — HEAD loads 152 KB `navigation.json` **inside `{% if parent_path %}`** with an `is defined` guard |
| ~39,039 × 18.8 MB deep clone per build | **INVALID for HEAD** |
| A avg 162.3 s / B avg 71.5 s / delta 90.8 s / 65.4 ms per page | **measures the withdrawn R2 variant only** |
| full-site projection ≈ 42.6 min | **INVALID for HEAD** |

## What still stands

- **Call-site topology**: `page_navigation::render(...)` is still invoked from `page.html` and
  `section.html`, so the macro is still reached by every leaf and section page. Only the **cost inside**
  the macro changed.
- **Coverage finding (323 routes)**: still valid — it is a property of the **data file**
  `data/page-navigation.json` vs the page corpus, independent of whether templates read it.
  All 323 missing are newer than the JSON (staleness, not a generation bug); 3 further routes
  (`xml-reference/bugs`) legitimately have no prev/next as singletons.

## ★ What this reading IS allowed to be used for (scope statement)

> **The A-side of this A/B is the R2 implementation (reading the 18.8 MB monolithic
> `page-navigation.json`). That implementation has been withdrawn.**
>
> **This reading's valid use is to inform the choice of DATA FORM for a future prev/next
> implementation.** Its conclusion there is clear and useful: **do not use a monolithic 18.8 MB
> file — use shards**, which serve both speed and freshness (all 323 missing routes are newer
> than the JSON, so a monolithic file re-stales on every batch of new pages).
>
> **It must NOT be used to support "the current state needs fixing".** The current committed state
> has no performance problem — it simply **does not have this feature**. The user shell removed
> prev/next deliberately (comment: scanning every sibling in Tera is quadratic and pushes a full
> build past GitHub's 6-hour limit).
>
> Without this scope note, the next reader will use it to fix a performance defect that does not exist.

## Why this happened (and it is the same failure I had just diagnosed elsewhere)

Earlier the same session I established that a **never-committed** ruler version (`3dc897bc`) had judged a
frozen batch. I then failed to apply that lesson here: I read the macro from the working tree, derived a
structural conclusion from it, and reported it — without checking `git status -- templates/` or reading
the **committed** version.

**Rule (Boss, adopted):** *once the carrier of a defect is withdrawn, every measurement of that carrier
is simultaneously invalidated. Before concluding anything about an implementation, confirm it is
**currently on disk and committed** — `git status -- <carrier>` plus reading the file itself.*
